#{[17592186366198 "Let $a=⟨4,2,-1⟩$, $b=⟨x,2,-1⟩$ and $|\\,a\\times b\\,|=\\sqrt{20}.$ Find $x.$"] [17592186367729 "If the solid region $R$ is given by \n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x-3)^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 9,\\,y\\le 0,\\,y\\le z\\le 6},\n$$\n\nthen $$\n\\displaystyle {∭}_{R}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{z+1}}\\,\\text{d}V=\n$$"] [17592186369399 "![](images/2a/2a89a14bced6f6700614cfad216070154cae165785458f817ea06d266edfde4e.png)\n\nUse Green's theorem to find the area of the region enclosed by the curve \n$C$ parametrized as $r(t)=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,i-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j$ \nfor $t\\in [0,\\pi ].$"] [17592186369406 "![](images/41/41dc9af596ca43b1d0d61bb4273be46d3a3d1adc62f99a70fe53c72d69667093.png)\n\nUse Green's theorem to find the area of the region enclosed by the curve \n$C$ parametrized as $r(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j$ \nfor $t\\in [\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{2}].$"] [17592186369413 "![](images/0d/0d988bfd18a945ba96c3556775d4aebc706252e2baf02d5f76c108e5534baed1.png)\n\nLet $D$ be the region enclosed by the curve $C$ parametrized as\n$r(t)=⟨{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t},\\,{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}⟩$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ].$ \nThe area of $D$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})dA={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\boxed{}\\,dt,\n$$\n\nwhere $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0.$ Determine the missing part of the equation.\n\n*Hint: Note that $C$ is negatively oriented.*"] [17592186369420 "![](images/81/81da86ae54a837b954bf93f8a75d93e1e7a8b64c93ba6f6b06cf74b2d8fa7ecb.png)\n\nLet $D$ be the region enclosed by the curve $C$ parametrized as\n$r(t)=⟨2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,3\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t⟩$ for $t\\in [0,\\pi ].$ \nThe area of $D$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})dA={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\boxed{}\\,dt,\n$$\n\nwhere $P(x,y)=0$ and $Q(x,y)=x.$ Determine the missing part of the equation.\n\n*Hint: Note that $C$ is negatively oriented.*"] [17592186369427 "![](images/9e/9eb614f4f6e00c5fa03759a61d69734aa1c6eaf26d98d3c78b96052ca16162d0.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2},$ where \n\n- ${C}_{1}$ is parametrized by ${r}_{1}(t)=-2{t}^{2}\\,i+3{t}^{3}\\,j$ for $t\\in [-1,1],$ and\n\n- ${C}_{2}$ is parametrized by ${r}_{2}(t)=-2\\,i-t\\,j$ for $t\\in [-3,3].$\n\nUse Green's theorem to find the area of the region."] [17592186369448 "![](images/52/52ec0dfc4590daf9306c2389bd321acbf80f263c799fa4ddd4e5b8f662e0a2b5.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2},$ where \n\n- ${C}_{1}$ is parametrized by ${r}_{1}(t)={t}^{5}\\,i+{t}^{4}\\,j$ for $t\\in [-1,1],$ and\n\n- ${C}_{2}$ is parametrized by ${r}_{2}(t)=-t\\,i+1\\,j$ for $t\\in [-1,1].$\n\nUse Green's theorem to find the area of the region."] [17592186369462 "![](images/49/49b14da54c952d0585f7aa894d618838ddc91beb65216e0c18c014ba5ad268d7.png)\n\nSuppose that $F$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ defined over the region $D$ above. Given that \n\n$$\n{∮}_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=9,\\,{∮}_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=-4,\\,{∮}_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=-6,\n$$\n\nwhere ${C}_{1}$ is oriented counterclockwise and ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ are oriented clockwise, calculate the value of $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{∂D}F\\cdot \\text{d}r.\n$$"] [17592186369469 "![](images/b3/b3562daf036c74c1472931a626d41f28de81e723377597700342823d7f2d1ac2.png)\n\nSuppose that $F$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ defined over the region $D$ above. Given that \n\n$$\n{∮}_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=1,\\,{∮}_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=7,\\,{∮}_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=-4,\n$$\n\nwhere ${C}_{1}$ is oriented counterclockwise and ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ are oriented clockwise, calculate the value of $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{∂D}F\\cdot \\text{d}r.\n$$"] [17592186369476 "![](images/00/00edab4b102cf705ea6c65acaa61ba524e9dba5b503167d6988b2150802d9f0b.png)\n\nSuppose that $F$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ defined over the region $D$ above. Given that \n\n$$\n{∮}_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=7,\\,{∮}_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=-9,\\,{∮}_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=1,\n$$\n\nwhere ${C}_{1}$ and ${C}_{3}$ are oriented counterclockwise and ${C}_{2}$ is oriented clockwise, calculate the value of $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{∂D}F\\cdot \\text{d}r.\n$$"] [17592186369483 "![](images/b2/b2754f286de30f2017974030e571ec52f85bb84ad633912922a2b514728f3a7b.png)\n\nSuppose that $F$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ defined over the region $D$ above. Given that \n\n$$\n{∮}_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=6,\\,{∮}_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=2,\\,{∮}_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=-10,\n$$\n\nwhere ${C}_{1}$ is oriented clockwise and ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ are oriented counterclockwise, calculate the value of $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{∂D}F\\cdot \\text{d}r.\n$$"] [17592186369490 "![](images/1f/1f308d1a3ecf3bd0090e22c4f70389311db4db3ff06f7038db0cf7770a8837c7.png)\n\nSuppose that $F$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ defined over the region $D$ above. Given that \n\n$$\n{∮}_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=5,\\,{∮}_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=-2,\\,{∮}_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=-9,\n$$\n\nwhere ${C}_{1}$ and ${C}_{2}$ are oriented clockwise and ${C}_{3}$ is oriented counterclockwise, calculate the value of $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{∂D}F\\cdot \\text{d}r.\n$$"] [17592186369511 "![](images/e6/e6252037001e1a5548ede86a16b4ddde8f5aec8fd956bded55a3506f7b533dbe.png)\n\nCalculate $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{∂D}(\\frac{1}{3}{y}^{3}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,dx+(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({sin}^{2}⁡y)-\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3})\\,dy,\n$$ where $D$ is the annular region shown above."] [17592186370913 "![](images/1d/1de9544b8da50f6280920adf2292da517a844b8b4143f58f646da0a01ffad16c.png)\n\nLet $D$ be the region enclosed by the curve $C$ parametrized as\n$r(t)=⟨3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ].$ The area of $D$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})dA={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\boxed{}\\,dt,\n$$\n\nwhere $P(x,y)=0$ and $Q(x,y)=x.$ Determine the missing part of the equation."] [17592186371721 "Find the arc length of the curve $r(t)=⟨t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩$\nfrom $t=0$ to $t=1.$"] [17592186373223 "Find the arc length of the curve $r(t)=⟨2-{t}^{2},\\,{t}^{2}+4,\\,\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}⟩$\nfrom  $t=0$ to $t=4.$"] [17592186373230 "Find the arc length of the curve $r(t)=⟨\\frac{4-\\sqrt{3}{t}^{2}}{\\sqrt{2}},\\,{t}^{3},\\,t+5⟩$\nfrom  $t=0$ to $t=5.$"] [17592186373965 "Consider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨z+x,\\,y+2z,\\,x-4y⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by $z=2x-4y$ for $-2\\le x\\le 0$ and $-1\\le y\\le 5.$ Using the parametrization $x=u$ and $y=v,$ the flux of $F$ through $S$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={\\int }_{-2}^{0}{\\int }_{-1}^{5}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}v\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression?"] [17592186373972 "Evaluate the surface integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}⟨y,\\,x+z,\\,z+2x⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S\n$$\n\nwhere the surface $S$ is defined by $z=y-2x$ for $1\\le x\\le 2$ and $0\\le y\\le 1.$"] [17592186373979 "For a surface $S$ given by $y=h(x,z)$ for $(x,z)\\in D,$ the flux of the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{D}(\\bbox[3pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{A}}\\,\\bbox[3pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{A}}+Q-R\\,\\bbox[3pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{A}})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFrom left to right, what are the missing parts of the formula?\n\n*Hint: The orientation of $S$ should be chosen such that the unit normal vector has a positive  $j$-component.*"] [17592186373986 "Consider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨2x-z,\\,y-z,\\,z+y⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by $z=4x-y$ for $0\\le x\\le 2$ and $-1\\le y\\le 2.$ Using the parametrization $x=u$ and $y=v,$ the flux of $F$ through $S$ can be expressed as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}v\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression?"] [17592186374186 "![](images/98/9861e9edc3c7517af6fd912046f62ec04a6e478942c3017ed9903e8b366130c2.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=⟨1-2x,\\,5y-3{z}^{2},\\,{x}^{3}-{y}^{2}⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by the bounding region of a right circular cylinder of height $2$ whose base is a circle of radius $3$ centered at the origin, as shown above. Given that $S$ is positively (outward) oriented, use the divergence theorem to find the flux of $F$ through $S.$"] [17592186374193 "![](images/07/07b46ab683e54813e0f6090c6841302ea7d914bc6b1577afc883f6e42e0a3903.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=⟨3x-y{z}^{2},\\,y+{e}^{z},\\,2y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by the bounding region of the rectangular solid shown above in the first octant. Given that $S$ is positively (outward) oriented, use the divergence theorem to find the flux of $F$ through $S.$"] [17592186374200 "Let $S$ be the boundary of the rectangular prism enclosed by the planes $x=1,$ $x=3,$ $y=0,$ $y=2,$ $z=-1,$ $z=1.$ Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $F=(y-{x}^{2})\\,i+xy\\,j+3xz\\,k,$ find the flux of $F$ across $S.$"] [17592186374207 "Let $S$ be the boundary of the rectangular prism enclosed by the planes $x=-1,$ $x=1,$ $y=0,$ $y=3,$ $z=1,$ $z=3.$ Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $F=(2{x}^{3}+z)\\,i-z\\,j-3{x}^{2}z\\,k,$ find the flux of $F$ across $S.$"] [17592186375881 "Find $c\\cdot (b\\times a),$ if $2a\\cdot ((-3b)\\times c)=10.$"] [17592186377076 "Let $a=⟨0,2,1⟩$, $b=⟨2,0,3⟩$ and $c=⟨1,4,5⟩.$ Find $a\\cdot (b\\times c)$ using a determinant.\n\n$a\\cdot (b\\times c)=$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186377079 "Find $a\\cdot (c\\times b)$, if $-3a\\cdot ((-2b)\\times (7c))=168.$\n\n$a\\cdot (c\\times b)=$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186380671 "Determine the domain of the vector field $F$ on ${R}^{2},$ given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-4}\\right)\\,i+\\frac{xy}{x-1}\\,j\\,.\n$$"] [17592186380685 "Determine which of the following shaded regions depicts the domain of the vector field $F$ on ${R}^{2},$ given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{y-x}}\\,i+\\sqrt{2-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}\\,j\\,.\n$$"] [17592186382847 "Consider the vector field $F=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)\\,i+y{z}^{2}\\,j+(x-z)\\,k.$ Evaluate $∇\\times F$ at the point  $(2,-1,3).$"] [17592186383034 "Consider the vector field $F=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)\\,i+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-y)\\,j.$ \nEvaluate $∇\\cdot F$ at the point $(0,-1).$"] [17592186383041 "Consider the vector field $F={x}^{2}yz\\,i+x{y}^{2}z\\,j+xy{z}^{2}\\,k.$ \nEvaluate $∇\\cdot F$ at the point $(1,2,2).$"] [17592186383055 "Consider the vector field $F=a\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x-y)\\,i+(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x-y)+1)\\,j$ on ${R}^{2}$, where $a$ is a constant. Given that $F$ is conservative, find the value of $a.$"] [17592186383062 "Consider the vector field $F(x,y)=(x+{y}^{2})\\,i+y\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)\\,j.$ Evaluate the scalar curl of $F$ at the point $(0,-1).$"] [17592186383069 "Consider the vector field $F(x,y)=(x-{y}^{2})\\,i+({y}^{3}-x)\\,j.$ Evaluate the scalar curl of $F$ at the point $(-1,1).$"] [17592186383076 "Given that $F(x,y)=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,i+(2x+3y)\\,j,$ what is the scalar curl of $F?$"] [17592186383244 "Consider the vector field $F=x{e}^{y}\\,i+xy{e}^{y}\\,j.$ \nEvaluate $∇\\cdot F$ at the point $(2,0).$"] [17592186383251 "Consider the vector field $F=x\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,i+y\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,j.$ \nEvaluate $∇\\cdot F$ at the point $(3,4).$"] [17592186383258 "Given that $F(x,y,z)=x\\,i+2y\\,j+3z\\,k,$ what is $\\text{curl}\\,F?$"] [17592186383265 "Consider the vector field $F={x}^{2}\\,i+xz\\,j+{z}^{2}\\,k.$ Evaluate $∇\\times F$ at the point  $(-2,-3,1).$"] [17592186383272 "Consider the vector field $F=(2xy+2z)\\,i+({y}^{2}+1)\\,j+(x+y)\\,k.$ Evaluate $∇\\times F$ at the point  $(-3,0,-1).$"] [17592186385415 "Consider the vector field $F=⟨{e}^{xz}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y,\\,0,\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡yz⟩.$ Find $\\text{div}\\,F.$"] [17592186385913 "Consider the vector field $F=xy{e}^{z}\\,j+yz{e}^{x}\\,k.$ Evaluate $∇\\times F$ at the point $(0,2,1).$"] [17592186387222 "Determine which of the following shaded regions depicts the domain of the vector field $F$ on ${R}^{2},$ given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}\\,i+\\frac{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}{x-y}\\,j\n$$"] [17592186387706 "![](images/00/008f5dbaeb8c3c26008c49e97ed0490c444b049a991c2198399359a30b806b07.png)\n\nConsider the path $C$ shown above. Given that $F(x,y)=⟨{e}^{x/4}+x{y}^{2},\\,{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡y}+2x⟩,$ which of the following is equivalent to $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr\\,?\n$$"] [17592186387803 "Consider the rectangle $C$ with vertices $(0,0),(3,0),(3,2),$ and $(0,2),$  traversed counterclockwise. Using the flux form of Green's theorem, express the flux of the vector field\n$F(x,y)={x}^{2}{y}^{2}\\,i+12xy\\,j$\nthrough $C$ as a repeated integral."] [17592186390360 "Let $a=⟨1,0,3⟩$ and $b=⟨2,5,3⟩.$ Find $a\\times b.$"] [17592186391631 "![](images/10/10ee30ddf508bf985e1c3a41491818723b57301412a5514e4a39c3bc13518acd.png)\n\nWhich integral  expression gives the arc length of the curve $g(t)=⟨t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,2-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩$\nfrom  $t=0$ to $t=\\pi ?$"] [17592186392884 "![](images/d4/d42e9c6ed71f06c2dec273a91d3a4cfbdf5c58095be9370285d7987d38f7b5a6.png)\n\nWhich integral  expression gives the arc length of the curve $h(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j+t\\,k$ from  $t=0$ to $t=2\\pi ?$"] [17592186392891 "Which integral expression gives the arc length of the curve $r(t)={t}^{3}\\,i+3t\\,j+{t}^{4}\\,k$ from  $t=2$ to $t=5?$"] [17592186394545 "If $$\n\\displaystyle D={(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{(y-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}\\le \\frac{1}{4},\\,x\\le 0},\n$$ then $$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A=\n$$"] [17592186394552 "If $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,(x-1)^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 1,\\,y\\le 0},$ then $$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}A=\n$$"] [17592186398235 "Determine the domain of the vector field $F$ on ${R}^{2},$ given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-{y}^{2})\\,i+({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}){e}^{1/x}\\,j\\,.\n$$"] [17592186398514 "Consider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨x+y,\\,2x-y,\\,x+z⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by $z=2x-2y$ for $0\\le x\\le 2$ and $-1\\le y\\le 2.$ Using the parametrization $x=u$ and $y=v,$ the flux of $F$ through $S$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}v\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression?"] [17592186398521 "Consider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨zy,\\,xz,\\,xy⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by $z=x-y$ for $-1\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 1.$ Using the parametrization $x=u$ and $y=v,$ the flux of $F$ through $S$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}v\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression?"] [17592186398562 "Let $S$ be the boundary of the rectangular prism enclosed by the planes $x=-1,$ $x=0,$ $y=1,$ $y=2,$ $z=0,$ $z=1.$ Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $F=xy\\,i+2y\\,j+{x}^{2}zk,$ the flux of $F$ across $S$ can be written as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing part of the expression?"] [17592186398569 "Let $S$ be the boundary of the rectangular prism enclosed by the planes $x=0,$ $x=4,$ $y=-1,$ $y=1,$ $z=0,$ $z=2.$ Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $F=({x}^{2}+y)\\,i-yz\\,j+4xz\\,k,$ the flux of $F$ across $S$ can be written as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\,\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing part of the expression?"] [17592186400750 "Let $u=⟨4,5,-1⟩$ and $v=⟨-6,0,2⟩.$ Find $u\\times v.$"] [17592186401240 "For a surface $S$ given by $y=2x{z}^{3}$ for $(x,z)\\in D,$ the flux of the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{D}(\\bbox[3pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{A}}\\,{z}^{3}+Q-\\bbox[3pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{A}}\\,x{z}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFrom left to right, what are the missing parts of the formula?\n\n*Hint: The orientation of $S$ should be chosen such that the unit normal vector has a positive  $j$-component.*"] [17592186401247 "For a surface $S$ given by $z=g(x,y)$ for $(x,y)\\in D,$ the flux of the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{\\bbox[3pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{A}}}{∂x}-\\bbox[3pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{A}}\\frac{∂g}{\\bbox[3pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{A}}}+R)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFrom left to right, what are the missing parts of the formula?"] [17592186401254 "For a surface $S$ given by $z=g(x,y)$ for $(x,y)\\in D,$ the flux of the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{\\bbox[3pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{A}}}-Q\\frac{∂g}{\\bbox[3pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{A}}}+R)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFrom left to right, what are the missing parts of the formula?"] [17592186401261 "Evaluate the surface integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}⟨\\frac{1}{z},\\,\\frac{xy}{z},\\,z⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S\n$$\n\nwhere the surface $S$ is defined by $z={e}^{x-y}$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le x.$"] [17592186407632 "Consider the vector field $F=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi x\\,i+y\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi y\\,j+{e}^{2z}\\,k.$ \nEvaluate $∇\\cdot F$ at the point $(0,1,0).$"] [17592186408071 "Let $r=⟨-4,-1,2⟩$, $q=⟨-2,3,1⟩$ and $p=⟨5,-2,-1⟩.$ Find $r\\cdot (q\\times p)$ using a determinant.\n\n$r\\cdot (q\\times p)=$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186408111 "Let $a=⟨-2,2,-3⟩$, $b=⟨2,-1,2⟩$ and $c=⟨-2,1,1⟩.$ Find $a\\cdot (b\\times c).$"] [17592186408112 "Let $a=⟨-1,2,4⟩$, $b=⟨0,3,1⟩$ and $c=⟨2,5,0⟩.$ Find $a\\cdot (b\\times c)$ using a determinant.\n\n$a\\cdot (b\\times c)=$\n\n$-15$"] [17592186409609 "Given that $$\n\\displaystyle F(x,y)=\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,i+\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,j,\n$$ what is the scalar curl of $F?$"] [17592186410422 "Find $c\\cdot (a\\times b),$ if $b\\times c=⟨4,3,-5⟩$ and $a=⟨-2,8,2⟩.$"] [17592186415273 "Consider the vector field $F(x,y)=\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+1}\\,i+\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+1}}\\,j.$ Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,\n$$ where $C$ is any smooth path from $(2,0)$ to $(3,-2)$ in ${R}^{2}.$"] [17592186415274 "Consider the vector field $F(x,y)=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,i+2y\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x\\,j.$ Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,\n$$ where $C$ is any smooth path from $(-1,1)$ to $(\\sqrt{3},2)$ in ${R}^{2}.$"] [17592186415276 "Consider the vector field $F(x,y)=6xy\\,i+3{x}^{2}\\,j.$ Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,\n$$ where $C$ is any smooth path from $(1,0)$ to $(2,-2)$ in ${R}^{2}.$"] [17592186415277 "Consider the vector field $F(x,y)={e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡y}\\,i+x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡y}\\,j.$ Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,\n$$ where $C$ is any smooth path from $(1,0)$ to $(2,\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ in ${R}^{2}.$"] [17592186415278 "Consider the scalar field $f(x,y,z)=z{e}^{x+y}.$ Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r,\n$$ where $C$ is any smooth path from $(-2,3,1)$ to $(0,1,-1)$ in ${R}^{3}.$"] [17592186415279 "Consider the scalar field $f(x,y,z)=x\\operatorname{arctan}⁡z-y.$ Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r,\n$$ where $C$ is any smooth path from $(1,0,1)$ to $(2,-2,0)$ in ${R}^{3}.$"] [17592186415280 "Consider the scalar field $f(x,y)=y{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.$ Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r,\n$$ where $C$ is any smooth path from $(0,2)$ to $(\\pi ,1)$ in ${R}^{2}.$"] [17592186416109 "![](images/e5/e54e2dc5c314ed1dda6ef9c60db5c5df3058ba120afec42bddbabc2622c3ae98.png)\n\nConsider the path $C$ shown above. Given that $F(x,y)=⟨x+y,\\,-xy⟩,$ which of the following is equivalent to $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr\\,?\n$$"] [17592186416110 "Use Green's theorem to evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr,\n$$ where $F(x,y)=⟨{sin}^{3}⁡x-{y}^{3},{y}^{4}+{x}^{3}⟩$ and $C$ is the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=9,$ oriented counterclockwise."] [17592186416111 "Given that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{4}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta ,\n$$\n\nwhere $F(x,y)=⟨y(x-1),\\,x(y-1)⟩$ and $C$ is the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=16$ oriented counterclockwise, what is the missing part of the equation?"]}
